  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 245 PM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nIn the long term we're finally going to see active weather as\ntroughing begins to take over the western US with an uptick in\nmoisture and instability fueled by the remnants of two tropical\nsystems lifting up from the Pacific. Over the weekend we'll see\nridging breaking down and moving off to the east as two lows\novertake the West Coast, one from the north and one from the\nsouth. These two systems will continue to merge bringing a large\nand deep cutoff upper level low that will control the Western \nUS into the start of the work week. As flow turns more \nsouthwesterly during the weekend, we'll see a notable influx of \nmoisture overspread the region. This should begin to fuel shower\nchances that will just barely move into the CWA on Saturday \nahead of a cold front which will slowly drop down across the \nregion through Sunday. Current forecast calls for precipitation \nto struggle to move further than the high terrain through Sunday\nas the bulk of the moisture remains confined further west, \nbringing stronger impacts across western and northern Wyoming. \nAlso of note, this initial push of colder air won't be nearly \nenough to completely shut down our October masquerading as \nsummer, with highs on Saturday still 12-18 degrees above normal \nas 700mb temperatures still remain around 10-12C on Saturday, \nand then slightly cooling on Sunday with highs still 8-12 \ndegrees above normal in the 60's to 70's.\n\nMonday into the middle of the week the closed upper level low\noff of California will begin to weaken by Tuesday and then \neject across the Rockies Tuesday into Wednesday, becoming \nabsorbed by upper level flow as another trough begins to then \ndevelop to bring further impacts just beyond the long term. \nWhile ridging will be able to amplify to our east across the \nMississippi River Valley, we'll remain in a split flow between \nthis and the troughing to our west. The NBM brings at least a \nlow-end chance of some precipitation on Monday and Tuesday as \nmoisture remains with PWAT values slightly above normal, but \nthis also means our temperatures should hover where they stood \non Sunday in the 60's to 70's. Wednesday is when we should \nfinally see cooler conditions and a bit more widespread \nprecipitation occur as the aforementioned West Coast low ejects \nacross the Rockies and just south of our region. While highs \nwill be a few degrees cooler and near normal to the west of the \nI-25 corridor, temperatures further east should still remain a \nfew degrees above normal in front of the cold front. But the \nlong term doesn't end without a shot of colder air, as the \novernight lows moving into Thursday morning should drop into the\n30's to 40's, with some locations getting their first freeze of\nthe season potentially. And with air this cool, the mountains \nshould finally dip low enough that snow will begin returning to \nthe higher terrain of the region. This could even spread down \ninto the adjacent valleys and lower terrain, but don't expect \nour first significant snow storm just yet as this will likely be\nmixed with rain and brief for the most part. But snowy peaks \nmay finally be on tap as we beginning moving into the second \nhalf of the month, and relief from our warm and dry conditions\nis expected.",
